Arcade Marée

Creative Catalyst: The genre bloomed in a ghost mall where teenagers rewired abandoned arcade cabinets.

Arcade Marée sounds like a Chet Baker trumpet solo filtered through a failing voltage regulator, set against the rhythmic stutter of mechanical joystick microswitches. It replaces the "cool jazz" walking bass with brittle, digital percussion captured via Toontrack Superior Drummer 3, where every hit is processed to sound like a ceramic CRT monitor shattering in a cathedral. The cinematic orchestral swells are not lush; they are claustrophobic, metallic, and frayed at the edges, sounding as if a full symphony were trapped inside the internal hum of a motherboard.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 110–135 BPM, characterized by "jitter-swing"—a rigid, programmed grid that intentionally drops frames or micro-stutters to mimic arcade hardware latency.
*   Harmonic Palette: Diminished jazz clusters blended with dissonant New Wave stabs; frequent use of Locrian mode to evoke the cold, artificial sterility of a defunct shopping center.
   Production Signatures: 1) Casing Re-Amping: Piping orchestral stems through a hollowed-out, vibrating cabinet shell to capture organic cabinet resonance. 2) Latency Quantization: Deliberately delaying the hi-hats by exactly 12ms to create a "ghostly" drag. 3) Data-Bit Clipping:* Using bit-crushers only on the trumpet reverb tails to make the sound "de-rez" into digital noise.
*   Dynamics: Tracks begin with isolated, intimate jazz textures, suddenly expanding into wall-of-sound orchestral crescendos that collapse into hollow, percussive silence.

Scene & Origin Lore
In the subterranean ruins of the Oakhaven Mall, the "Cabinet Kids" don’t just play games; they perform surgery on them. Wearing repurposed security guard uniforms adorned with motherboard circuitry and tarnished brass buttons, they gather around a central altar of twelve wired-together Street Fighter II cabinets. Rituals involve syncing the MIDI-triggered drums with the rhythmic blinking of arcade marquees. The fashion is "Hollow-Lux": oversized wool coats meant for outdoor weather paired with protective lead-lined eyewear, symbolizing the transition from the sterile light of the screens to the decaying darkness of the mall floor.

Typical Instruments: Muted trumpet, Toontrack Superior Drummer 3 (heavily triggered with metallic samples), analog square-wave oscillators, field-recorded arcade mechanical clicks, and sampled chamber-string ensembles.

For Fans Of: Miles Davis’ Bitches Brew (reimagined as a motherboard failure), Joy Division, Ennio Morricone, Aphex Twin.

